<h2>This isn't really about definitions</h2>
<p>Most Incoterms explainers list the four terms and move on. That's useful reference material — <a href="/resources/incoterms-guide/">we have one too</a> — but it doesn't answer the question that actually matters to a buyer: which one should <em>you</em> choose? That depends less on the definitions and more on your own logistics situation.</p>

<h2>The real decision factors</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Do you have an existing freight forwarder relationship?</strong> If yes, FOB usually gets you the lowest landed cost, since you're likely getting better freight rates than a new customer would from any single exporter's arranged freight.</li>
  <li><strong>Is this your first order from India?</strong> CIF reduces the number of moving parts you have to manage on a first transaction — the seller handles freight and insurance, and you just need to handle customs clearance at your end.</li>
  <li><strong>How much control do you want over routing and carrier choice?</strong> EXW gives you the most control but requires the most logistics capability on your side, starting from the seller's warehouse door.</li>
  <li><strong>Do you want insurance handled separately, or bundled?</strong> CFR bundles freight into the seller's price but leaves insurance to you — useful if you already have a marine cargo insurance policy that covers all your shipments regardless of origin.</li>
</ul>

<h2>A simple framework</h2>
<table class="data-table" style="margin-block:1em">
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Your situation</th><th scope="col">Consider</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>First order, no existing forwarder</td><td>CIF</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Established forwarder relationship, want lowest cost</td><td>FOB</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Have your own cargo insurance policy</td><td>CFR</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Want full control from India onward</td><td>EXW</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>What doesn't change between terms</h2>
<p>Regardless of Incoterm, the product specification, documentation requirements and payment terms are separate negotiations — the Incoterm only governs shipping responsibility and risk transfer point, not quality or payment. Don't let an Incoterm conversation substitute for agreeing the things that actually determine whether you're happy with what arrives.</p>

<h2>Ask, don't guess</h2>
<p>If you're genuinely unsure, the fastest path is telling your supplier your destination port and whether you have a forwarder relationship, and asking for a recommendation. A supplier who regularly ships your route should have a clear, specific answer rather than a generic "it depends."</p>

<h2>One more factor worth weighing: cash flow timing</h2>
<p>Beyond cost and control, Incoterms affect when in the shipping process money changes hands and risk transfers, which matters for cash flow planning on larger orders. Under FOB and EXW, you're typically managing your own freight payment on your own schedule with your carrier. Under CIF and CFR, freight cost is bundled into what you pay the exporter, which can simplify budgeting even if it doesn't change the total landed cost meaningfully. Neither approach is inherently better — it depends on how your business prefers to structure payment timing across a shipment's lifecycle, and it's a reasonable factor to raise alongside the logistics-control question when deciding.</p>
